      "evidence": "The paragraph asserts: \"Two conditions $(t_1,T_1)$, $(t_2,T_2)$ with $|t_1|\\le|t_2|$ are compatible exactly when $t_1=t_2\\cap 2^{\\le|t_1|}$, and then the downward closure of $T_1\\cup T_2$ is again a perfect nowhere-dense tree whose initial tree through height $|t_2|$ is $t_2$, so $(t_2,T_1\\cup T_2)$ is a common strengthening.\" Counterexample with the item's own order ($T_1\\subseteq T_2$ and $t_1=t_2\\cap2^{\\le|t_1|}$): let $T_1=\\{\\sigma\\in2^{<\\omega}:\\sigma$ contains no two consecutive $0$s$\\}$, $t_1=T_1\\cap2^{\\le1}=\\{\\emptyset,0,1\\}$, and $T_2=\\{\\sigma:\\sigma$ contains no two consecutive $1$s$\\}$, $t_2=T_2\\cap2^{\\le2}=\\{\\emptyset,0,1,00,01,10\\}$. Both trees are perfect with nowhere dense bodies, $|t_1|=1\\le2=|t_2|$ and $t_2\\cap2^{\\le1}=t_1$, so the stated criterion declares the two conditions compatible. But any common strengthening $(\\tilde t,\\tilde T)$ has $\\tilde T\\supseteq T_1\\ni 11$, so if $|\\tilde t|\\ge2$ then $11\\in\\tilde t\\cap2^{\\le2}=t_2$, contradicting $11\\notin t_2$ (the string $11$ has two consecutive $1$s); and $|\\tilde t|<2$ cannot have $\\tilde t\\cap2^{\\le2}=t_2$ since $t_2$ has height $2$. Hence they are incompatible, and $(t_2,T_1\\cup T_2)$ is not even a condition below $(t_2,T_2)$, because $(T_1\\cup T_2)\\cap2^{\\le2}\\ni11\\notin t_2$. (The one-directional facts in the same paragraph -- necessary agreement on the common levels, pairwise compatibility of conditions with one fixed recorded tree, and the resulting countable union of directed classes -- are correct and the ccc conclusion is unaffected; the defect is the stated biconditional and the \"common strengthening\" conclusion built on it.)",
